From 1ab5820518e6a8d132e08bf1855d9c4bd282d4f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CounterStrikeXB2 <cs2@local>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 06:00:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] recomp: register promotion (--promote-regs) (CounterStrikeXB2
 local patch)

The guest registers are thread-local globals (g_eax ...), so every register
access in a translated function was a memory access, and because guest memory
is reached through volatile pointers MSVC also reloaded the thread-local block
after every guest store: Counter-Strike's collision trace (sub_000B7E80) did
245 thread-local loads and 2414 memory operands in 3751 instructions.

With --promote-regs each translated function loads the registers into locals
on entry (RECOMP_REGS_DECL), writes them back immediately before every transfer
out -- direct and indirect calls, indirect tail jumps, direct tail and
fall-through calls, a guest longjmp, and return (RECOMP_REGS_SPILL) -- and reads
them back after each call (RECOMP_REGS_FILL). The write-back sits between the
return-address push and the call, and each insertion is braced so a call under
a bare if stays one statement. The runtime header defines the hooks only under
RECOMP_REG_PROMOTE; otherwise they are no-ops and eax is g_eax, so one
generated tree builds either way. Kernel bridges, interrupts at their safe
points and the ICALL failure paths only run inside a call, where the globals
are current; the ICALL failure paths now write g_eax so the read-back after
the call keeps their 0. A setjmp that returns through a guest longjmp reads
the registers back before taking the return value.

Without the option the output is unchanged (full test suite passes). Same
function after promotion: 8 thread-local loads, 1455 memory operands, 2709
instructions. Counter-Strike: round-start dips gone (worst frame 35 ms, was
30-42 ms bursts), steady 14 ms frames.
